# NAVBAR — QuantFun Web

## Objective

Implement the navigation bar for the QuantFun institutional portfolio website.

The navbar should feel calm, premium, architectural, and almost invisible until users need it.

It should never feel like a SaaS dashboard or startup landing page.

The overall aesthetic follows:

- Editorial Magazine
- Contemporary Museum
- Luxury Annual Report
- Institutional Research
- Swiss Minimalism

The attached homepage design is the primary visual reference and should be followed as closely as possible.

---

# Layout

Desktop only for now.

Height:
Approximately 72px.

Content alignment:

Logo on the left, navigation right-aligned.

No CTA button.

No border.

No background when page first loads.

Everything should appear extremely lightweight.

Page is constrained to 260px padding on left and right.

---

# Position

Navbar must be:

position: fixed

Always stay on top while scrolling.

Use smooth transitions.

When scrolling, the navbar should never jump.

---

# Background Behavior

Initial state:

Transparent.

No blur.

No shadow.

No border.

As users scroll:

Keep background transparent.

Do NOT introduce glassmorphism.

Do NOT use colored backgrounds.

The website should feel clean and editorial.

---

# Logo

Use the existing QuantFun logo in the public/img/web-logo.png.

Final size: 150x92px (20% smaller than original Figma, then reduced further 10%).

Keep original proportions.

Margin top: 10px.

Padding left should feel generous (aligned with 260px page padding).

---

# Navigation Items

About

Portfolio

Research

Network

Typography:

Font: Inter

Weight: 400

Size: 13px

Letter spacing: 0.04em (very subtle)

No uppercase.

Opacity: 80% normal, 100% hover.

Spacing between menu items: 209px.

---

# Hover Interaction

Hover scale: 125%.

Opacity transition: 80% → 100%.

Transition: 250ms ease.

No underline.

No background.

No animated indicator.

---

# Adaptive Text Color

Not implemented. Text remains #111111 (black) at all times.

---

# Responsive Behavior

Desktop first.

Mobile navigation will be implemented later.

No hamburger menu yet.

---

# Motion

Subtle.

Professional.

Museum-like.

Never playful.

Transitions should feel almost invisible.

---

# Accessibility

Navigation must remain keyboard accessible.

Visible focus state.

Proper semantic HTML: <nav> <ul> <li> <a>

Use aria-current for active navigation.

---

# Component Architecture

components/layout/Navbar.tsx

---

# Coding Principles

- Use TypeScript.
- Use Tailwind CSS.
- No inline styles.
- Reusable.
- Clean component separation.
- Minimal dependencies.
- Avoid unnecessary re-renders.

---

# Visual Priority

The navbar should disappear visually into the design.

Users should notice the content first.

The navigation should quietly support the experience rather than becoming the focal point.

Think of Apple, BlackRock, modern museums, and architecture studios—not startup landing pages.

---

